EN3B = {
"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel": ("Mangal Pandey and 1857", "Mangal Pandey is remembered as one of the first heroes of the great revolt of 1857. He was born on 19 July 1827 in Nagwa, a village in the Ballia district of present day Uttar Pradesh. He joined the army of the East India Company as a sepoy in the 34th Bengal Native Infantry. At that time, there was deep anger among Indian soldiers over low pay, unfair treatment and the fear that the new Enfield rifle cartridges were greased with the fat of cows and pigs. On 29 March 1857, at Barrackpore near Calcutta, Mangal Pandey openly rose against his British officers and called upon his fellow soldiers to join him. He was arrested and hanged on 8 April 1857. His sacrifice lit a spark that soon spread to Meerut, Delhi, Kanpur, Lucknow and Jhansi. Although the revolt was suppressed, it shook the foundations of Company rule and inspired generations of freedom fighters."),
"Holi, the Festival of Colours": ("Ganesh Chaturthi", "Ganesh Chaturthi is celebrated with great devotion in many parts of India, especially in Maharashtra, Karnataka, Goa and Andhra Pradesh. It marks the birth of Lord Ganesha, who is worshipped as the remover of obstacles and the god of wisdom. The festival begins on the fourth day of the bright half of the month of Bhadrapad and continues for up to ten days. Families bring home beautiful idols of Ganesha, decorate them with flowers and offer modak, his favourite sweet. Huge public pandals are set up in cities, where cultural programmes, music and social awareness activities are organised. It was Bal Gangadhar Tilak who, in 1893, turned this festival into a public celebration to bring people together against British rule. On the last day, known as Anant Chaturdashi, the idols are carried in grand processions for immersion. Many people now prefer eco friendly idols made of clay, so that rivers and lakes are not polluted."),
"Raksha Bandhan": ("Lokmanya Tilak", "Bal Gangadhar Tilak was born on 23 July 1856 in Ratnagiri, Maharashtra. He was a brilliant scholar of mathematics and Sanskrit, a teacher, a journalist and one of the most powerful leaders of the early freedom movement. Through his newspapers, Kesari in Marathi and Mahratta in English, he criticised British policies fearlessly and awakened national pride among the people. He gave the famous call, Swaraj is my birthright and I shall have it, which became a war cry for the whole country. Tilak used festivals like Ganesh Chaturthi and Shivaji Jayanti to bring people together for political awakening. He was imprisoned several times, and during his long imprisonment in Mandalay, he wrote the famous book Gita Rahasya. He also helped found the Deccan Education Society to spread modern education. The people lovingly called him Lokmanya, which means one who is accepted by the people. He passed away on 1 August 1920."),
"Dr. A. P. J. Abdul Kalam": ("Onam, the Harvest Festival", "Onam is the most important festival of Kerala and is celebrated by people of all communities with great enthusiasm. It falls in the Malayalam month of Chingam, which comes in August or September, and marks the end of the monsoon and the harvest of rice. According to legend, Onam celebrates the annual homecoming of King Mahabali, a generous ruler under whom the people lived in equality and happiness. The celebrations last for ten days. Women draw colourful floral designs called pookalam in front of their homes, adding a new ring of flowers each day. The highlight of the festival is the Onam Sadya, a grand vegetarian feast served on banana leaves, with more than twenty dishes. Boat races, especially the famous snake boat races, attract huge crowds along the backwaters. Folk dances such as Thiruvathirakali and Pulikali add colour to the celebrations. Onam reflects the spirit of unity and prosperity that Kerala cherishes."),
"Lal Bahadur Shastri": ("Rabindranath Tagore", "Rabindranath Tagore was born on 7 May 1861 in Calcutta. He was a poet, novelist, painter, musician, educationist and philosopher, and he is often called Gurudev. He began writing poems at a very young age, and over his lifetime he composed thousands of songs, which are known as Rabindra Sangeet. In 1913, he became the first Asian to win the Nobel Prize in Literature for his collection of poems, Gitanjali. Tagore had strong views on education. He believed that children should learn in close contact with nature, in an atmosphere of freedom and creativity. With this idea, he founded a school at Santiniketan, which later grew into Visva Bharati University. He wrote the national anthem of India, Jana Gana Mana, and also the national anthem of Bangladesh. When British troops fired on unarmed people at Jallianwala Bagh in 1919, he returned his knighthood in protest. He passed away on 7 August 1941."),
"Chandra Shekhar Azad": ("Lala Lajpat Rai", "Lala Lajpat Rai, popularly known as Punjab Kesari or the Lion of Punjab, was born on 28 January 1865 in Dhudike in Punjab. He was a lawyer, writer and social reformer, and together with Bal Gangadhar Tilak and Bipin Chandra Pal, he formed the famous trio known as Lal Bal Pal. He played an important role in founding the Punjab National Bank and the Lakshmi Insurance Company, and he supported the spread of education through the Dayanand Anglo Vedic schools. In 1928, the British government sent the Simon Commission to India to suggest constitutional reforms, but it did not have a single Indian member. When the Commission reached Lahore on 30 October 1928, Lala ji led a peaceful protest against it. The police attacked the protesters with lathis, and he was seriously injured. He died on 17 November 1928. His words, that every blow on his body would be a nail in the coffin of British rule, inspired many young revolutionaries."),
}
